This work-only candidate is a read-only public evidence verifier for the reviewed Notes inline-confirmation installation. It imports only Node filesystem/path/assert/crypto modules and executes no application, original helper, GUI, host, network, fixture archive or benchmark. Root owns installation/publication and behavioral acceptance.

Copy only `verify-notes-inline-followups.mjs` to the repository's `scripts/` when root approves its source-bound receipt. It has no path relative to this stage. The root argument is the public repository directory, not Studio or a work directory. The public layout is exactly868 inert aliases under `products/rea-studio/apps/notes/results/inline-confirmation-followup/`: the original818 public map rows, separate18 addendum files, and separate32 qualification-correction files. The eleven local fixture exclusions are authenticated as records without reading private original work paths or native carriers. All seven original strict manifests resolve via aliases. The separate31-file correction has its own exact manifest and does not replace the historical map.

`verifyNotesInlineFollowups(root, {requireCurrent = true, onFile})` checks exact aliases/maps, source epochs,31 source/license prerequisites,543-byte notice alias, source-ID/SHA159 and capability-ID232 projections, eight proposals, two client substitutions, optional import-only reconstructions and authenticated browser8 scope. Current registry whole-file hashes are not forced to equal old observations. Each input is individually bounded at32MiB, aggregate unique inputs at512MiB/5,000 paths; inventory traversal has a40-level limit. No race-free filesystem security or hard I/O deadline is asserted. `onFile` receives one `{path, bytes, sha256}` for each new authenticated input; the verifier rechecks reads at completion.

The default `requireCurrent: true` verifies six exact current replacements,28 unchanged paths and two new destinations. `requireCurrent: false` authenticates immutable evidence and prospective source proposals without reading any live application destination; it explicitly returns `currentBytesChecked: false`. Both modes return `installedBehaviorAcceptance: false`: exact installed bytes do not prove a new current-layout test campaign. Browser/original execution counts remain zero for this verifier. The separate existing browser receipt authenticates eight finite groups, not fresh replay or complete product acceptance. Its upload limitation, permission state, download-event timeout and programmatic fixture seed remain explicit.

The return includes a `resolveHistorical(destination, expectedHistoricalIdentity)` closure, also available through `createNotesInlineHistoricalResolver(root, options)`. Root can create it once for the older34 installation verifier. For each of the34 exact historical destinations, it returns `{historicalPath, historicalIdentity, currentExpected, mode}` where `historicalPath` points to the immutable old before-image alias. The supplied identity must exactly equal the old receipt identity. The result is cloned; mutating it cannot alter the mapping. Six current identities are explicit later replacements and28 stay exact. An unrelated path or either new destination returns `undefined`; the two new destinations are verified separately in the eight proposals. This never rebinds the old receipt to current bytes.

`applyNotesInlineQualificationOverrides(baseBytes, override)` is a pure source-only projection: it permits exactly twelve corrected-browser `qualification` strings, rejects row duplication/omission and any identity or other-field change, and leaves six older independent-review images unchanged. The caller pins the original map, override, correction manifest and reconstructed full map before applying it. All source/image bytes and `.png` names remain exact even where JPEG headers identify the true media type.

The first two owned collector failures are retained: a syntax error before any fixture action, and selecting the six older images by their client-source label rather than actual `independent-negative` evidence epoch. They are verification-tool mistakes, not product faults. The initial836 version passed nineteen source-only controls at module SHA13904748eb6a02e711c6ec4736a0270a2e7f14c6b244ddb5ff2936d1327d8916. Its source/raw/receipt remain exact. The final868 version passed twenty-three controls with source before/after exact: prospective/current separation, historical34 mapping, unsafe paths, native leading signatures, altered public/current bytes, unlisted aliases and four qualification-policy negatives. Controls manipulate only an owned private copy. They execute no application or original implementation and infer no new browser acceptance.

Generated private layouts remain local verification fixtures. Their exact copy maps are preserved; they are not a proposed public stage tree. The proposed module and its own receipts/source/diagnostic records are separate from those mirrors. No excluded JEX or attachment fixture was copied. Full product parity, security equivalence, legal compliance and superiority remain false or unestablished.
